\description{
Computes the lymph node ratio (LNR = positive nodes / examined nodes) for
each patient, assesses nodal-yield adequacy against a minimum-examined
threshold, and relates LNR to a survival outcome. LNR can be stratified
either by established category thresholds or by a data-driven optimal
cutpoint chosen to maximize the log-rank separation, and the resulting
strata are compared with Kaplan-Meier / log-rank testing. LNR is an
established prognostic factor across colorectal, gastric, breast, and
head-and-neck cancers, and is more robust than absolute node counts when
nodal yield varies between specimens.
}
